{
  "_comment": "OPT-IN, the owner's call. Rendered by /bajzi:night-run to ~/night-runs/<project>/settings.local.json, then installed by the OWNER in PHASE E as <BASE>/.claude/settings.local.json (PHASE E mkdir -p's the .claude folder and BACKS UP any file already there). Claude Code loads it as project settings for every session started in that worktree, which is exactly the per-story night sessions: the allow list skips the auto-mode classifier for these command shapes, the deny list blocks these shapes outright, and everything else still goes through the classifier. Deleting the file reverts the machine to plain auto mode; if PHASE E printed a settings.local.json.pre-night-<stamp> backup, move THAT one back instead of just deleting.",
  "_comment_deny_form": "CORRECTNESS FIX vs the hand-written original: file rules are written as Edit(<glob>), NOT Write(<glob>). Verified against Claude Code 2.1.276, whose own warning reads: Write(...) rules are not consulted by the file permission checks - only Edit(path) rules are. A Write-only deny therefore silently permits the edit it was meant to block; an Edit(<glob>) rule covers ALL file-editing tools for that path, and also the file-naming Bash commands (cat/head/tail/sed/tee) and shell redirections into that path. Keep every path rule below in the Edit(...) form.",
  "_comment_matching": "HOW Bash(...) RULES MATCH - THIS PARAGRAPH IS ABOUT Bash(...) RULES ONLY (verified against 2.1.276). Edit(...) and Read(...) path rules are matched by a DIFFERENT, gitignore-style matcher and NOTHING here applies to them: see GLOB GRAMMAR in _comment_symlinks below, where '*' does not cross '/' - measured, Edit(d2/*.txt) refused d2/a.txt but ALLOWED the write to d2/sub/b.txt, while the Bash transform described here would have denied it. Never carry a shape from one matcher to the other. A Bash rule is a STRING pattern over the command text the session writes; every '*' is expanded to '.*' and the whole pattern is anchored, so '*' works at the START, in the MIDDLE and at the end, and a trailing ':*' is the same as ' *'. There is NO path, alias or quoting normalization - the pattern is matched against the RAW command text, quotes included. MEASURED 2026-09-18: with 'Bash(/usr/bin/git -C <dir>/wt/* rev-parse *)' denied, the UNQUOTED 'git -C <dir>/wt/S1 rev-parse ...' was refused while the QUOTED 'git -C \"<dir>/wt/S1\" rev-parse ...' fell through untouched - that is why every scoped '-C' rule below keeps a '*' immediately after '-C ', to absorb the opening quote the rendered BRIEF writes. Likewise Bash(/usr/bin/git push:*) does NOT match 'git push ...', and neither of them matches '/usr/bin/git -C <dir> push ...', because '-C <dir>' sits between the two words. So: ALLOW rules spell the exact forms the rendered BRIEF tells the session to use, with '*' where the brief has a path; DENY rules carry a leading and a trailing '*' AND a '*' between 'git' and the verb, so they catch every spelling: the bare 'git' one, the '/usr/bin/git' one and the '-C <dir>' one - '*git push*--force*' misses 'git -C /x push --force' because the two words are not adjacent there, which is exactly how a 3am session would slip past it. A command joined with && ; or | is split and every part is matched separately, and DENY always beats ALLOW.",
  "_comment_limits": "WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT DO. Prefix matching has no flag analysis, so an allow rule can never constrain what comes AFTER it: Bash(gh pr merge:*) auto-approves '--admin' and '-R other/repo', and Bash(/usr/bin/git push:*) auto-approves 'git push origin HEAD:<base>', which bypasses the PR, the review loop and CI. Note also that a rule may NOT end in ':*' when the colon is meant literally - ':*' IS the space-wildcard suffix syntax, so 'Bash(*git*push*:*)' means 'any push at all' and would deny every legitimate push; the refspec denies below therefore name the base branch after the colon. That is why neither of those open-ended rules exists below, why every push allow ends in a branch shape, and why the dangerous flag shapes are spelled out in deny. Anything NOT matched here is not denied - it falls through to the auto-mode classifier - so a tight allow list costs nothing and a wide one costs everything. Never widen a rule to silence a 3am prompt. This file is the outermost of three fences; the rendered BRIEF and the Opus review-and-fix loop are the other two.",
  "_comment_placeholders": "Replace every <angle-bracket> value; /bajzi:night-run PHASE C renders them all and leaves NONE behind: <BASE_BRANCH> and <BRANCH_PREFIX> from config.env, <project> from PROJECT, <NIGHT_DIR> and <BASE_DIR> from config.env's NIGHT_DIR and BASE - both as ABSOLUTE paths with no trailing slash and no '~' (a Bash rule matches raw command text, so a '~' in the rule would only ever match a literal tilde). The '-C' allow rules are deliberately scoped to those two trees: an unscoped 'git -C *' reached EVERY git repo on this shared machine (measured 2026-09-18: with 'Bash(/usr/bin/git -C * symbolic-ref *)' as a deny rule, a repo entirely outside the run's trees was still matched, while the scoped shape was not), which is a metadata-write hole - staging, committing, pushing or adding a worktree in a neighbouring project's repo. Then add one Edit(...) deny line per forbidden path from docs/NIGHT-RULES.md section 3 and one Read(...) deny line per secret file - that translation is section 3's ONLY enforcement channel. PATH SYNTAX: a single leading '/' is resolved relative to THIS settings file's project directory (the BASE worktree), so an absolute path written that way silently matches nothing; write home paths as '~/...' and anything outside home with a DOUBLED leading slash ('//etc/**'). Remove any line that does not apply - a rule naming a path this project does not have is noise, not safety.",
  "_comment_symlinks": "PATH DENY RULES AND SYMLINKS - MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED (Claude Code 2.1.276, 2026-09-18; scratch fixture + headless `claude -p --permission-mode acceptEdits` runs, every claim checked against the file on disk afterwards, with an untouched control file in the same directory proving the writes were really happening). (1) FORWARD: a write aimed at a SYMLINK is denied by a rule naming either the link or its target - the checker expands the path it is handed through every symlink hop and through realpath, and denies if ANY of those spellings matches a deny rule. (2) REVERSE: a rule that names a symlink ALSO covers the file that link currently points at, because every ROOTED deny pattern gets a 'physical twin' pattern compiled beside it - the rule's own path resolved through symlinks. Measured: with Edit(~/d/state.txt) denied and state.txt -> state-2026-09-18.txt, a Write to state-2026-09-18.txt was refused with 'File is in a directory that is denied by your permission settings.'; deleting the symlink and repeating the identical run ALLOWED the very same write. (An unrooted, single-segment rule such as Edit(linkB.txt) did NOT get that twin - reverse cover is a property of rooted '~/...' rules only.) (3) NEVER LEAN ON (2). The twin is only the link's CURRENT target and it is resolved once when the rules compile, so it is stale the moment run.sh repoints the link at a new night's file, and it never covers the PREVIOUS night's state-before-<date>.txt. Name the real files instead. THAT IS WHY THE STATE RULE BELOW IS state*.txt AND NOT state.txt: run.sh writes state-<RUN_DATE>.txt, keeps state.txt only as a SYMLINK to it, and preserves an older real state.txt as state-before-<RUN_DATE>.txt - and the rendered BRIEF puts the REAL per-run name in front of the story session, so a fence naming only state.txt is a fence the session walks straight past. GLOB GRAMMAR: a path rule is matched by a gitignore-style matcher, NOT by the Bash rule transform described above - '*' here does not cross '/', so state*.txt covers state.txt, state-<date>.txt and state-before-<date>.txt in that ONE directory and nothing deeper. Measured with no symlink present at all: Edit(~/d/state*.txt) refused writes to both state-<date>.txt and state-before-<date>.txt while other.txt in the same directory was written. Do not carry assumptions from the Bash rules to the path rules or back. SECOND GUARD: BRIEF-<id>.md has one - run.sh chmod 0444's it the moment it is rendered, independently of any rule. The STATE FILE HAS NO EQUIVALENT: the runner appends a row to it after every story, so it must stay writable, and this deny rule is its ONLY guard. Weaken it and nothing else catches the write.",
